# Dev image pins — what the `construct-server-dev` project runs (SERV-97).
#
# The dev counterpart of `versions.env`, and deliberately NOT the same file. Dev's whole
# job is to run the code that just merged, so every value here is `latest` and that is the
# design rather than a placeholder waiting to be filled in.
#
# ── Then why does the file exist at all? ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Three reasons, none of which need dev to stop floating:
#
#   1. It gives a temporary dev pin a home. "Hold dev at switchyard 4.7 while I reproduce
#      a bug" is a normal request, and the answer should be a reviewable one-line diff that
#      the next deploy re-applies — not an edit to the deployed `.env`, which
#      `render-env.sh` regenerates and discards without warning.
#   2. It keeps dev's tag vocabulary structurally separate from prod's. See below.
#   3. It makes dev's version state answerable the same way prod's is — from git, not by
#      shelling into the box.
#
# ── Why DEV_ prefixes, rather than reusing SWITCHYARD_TAG ───────────────────────
# Sharing prod's variable names would work, right up until prod's `.env` ended up at the
# dev root — at which point dev would silently run prod's pinned versions and look fine
# doing it. Distinct names make that failure inert: with no `DEV_*_TAG` set, the `:-latest`
# fallback in `docker-compose.dev.yml` applies and dev floats, which is dev's intended
# state anyway. It also matches the existing convention in that file, where everything
# dev-specific is already `DEV_`-prefixed (`DEV_SWITCHYARD_PORT`, `DEV_LYCEUM_AUTH`).
#
# Copying the prod `.env` into the dev root remains forbidden for much larger reasons
# (SERV-77: a dev purser holding prod credentials provisions real accounts in real
# services). This is a second line, not the first.
#
# ── One variable per SOURCE REPO ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same convention as versions.env: switchyard's backend and frontend ship from one release
# and pin together, so four variables cover five images.
#
# ── How this reaches dev ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `deploy-dev.yml` renders this file and the DEV_ENV_FILE secret into a single
# `/opt/construct-server-dev/.env` via `scripts/render-env.sh` — the same script prod uses,
# for the same reason: the deployed `.env` has to stay the complete environment, so the
# `make dev-*` targets and a bare compose invocation on the box all resolve the same
# values.
#
# NOTE that `latest` does not move on its own. A floating TAG is not a floating CONTAINER:
# compose compares the image string, which never changes here, so dev only moves when
# something pulls. That is `deploy-dev.yml`, and it is why the workflow pulls before it
# ups rather than relying on `up -d` to notice.

DEV_ARGOSY_TAG=latest
DEV_LYCEUM_TAG=latest
DEV_PURSER_TAG=latest
DEV_SWITCHYARD_TAG=latest
